Let's get back to the bug and the actual cause so we know where to steer this:

%% Bug report: After saving changes to a patient's care plan, the dashboard occasionally shows the old care plan. 
%% Refreshing the page fixes it. Reported by 3 nurses on the night shift.
%% Root cause: Even though a cache invalidation job checks the main plan table's last_updated_at field, there is a race condition...?

Yes — steer it toward the refresh clue.

The original “updated_at never changes, so cache keeps serving old plan” cause does not explain why refresh fixes it. A race condition can.

A better root-cause shape might be:

Save updates care_plan_notes.
Dashboard cache/read model is refreshed asynchronously.
Immediately after save, dashboard may still read the old cached/read-model value.
A page refresh happens slightly later, after the async invalidation/refresh has completed, so it shows the updated care plan.
So the likely causal chain is:



So instead of steering toward:

cache invalidation never happens

steer toward:

cache invalidation/refresh happens asynchronously and is not complete when the dashboard first reloads.


Those comments I gave you are at the top of the file and are meant to keep the investigation modeling on track. Can we rephrase them and be more specific about a race condition that could cause this?

Yes. I would make the comments describe the scenario precisely enough that the investigation has a definite destination, without revealing how the investigators should discover it.

For example:

%% Bug report:
%% After saving changes to a patient's care plan, the dashboard occasionally
%% shows the old care plan. Refreshing the page a few seconds later shows the
%% updated care plan. Reported by 3 nurses on the night shift.

%% Root cause:
%% Saving the care plan triggers an asynchronous cache invalidation/update
%% process. The save request returns before the invalidation completes, creating
%% a race condition between the dashboard reading cached data and the cache being
%% refreshed. If the dashboard reloads before invalidation finishes, it displays
%% stale data. A later refresh succeeds because the cache has since been updated.
I like this better than the original because:

It explains why the bug is intermittent ("occasionally").

It explains why refresh fixes it (time has elapsed).

It identifies the race condition explicitly: dashboard read vs. asynchronous cache refresh.

It doesn't bake in a specific implementation like updated_at, so your investigation can naturally discover whether the race is caused by a timestamp check, a delayed job, a queue, or some other asynchronous mechanism.

That gives the investigation more room to uncover the specific implementation details while still converging on the correct class of root cause.


